Under current NC Medicaid CAP/DA policy, the general eligibility criteria are that the person must be enrolled in NC Medicaid, meet the policy’s general medical-necessity-style coverage standards, and meet CAP/DA-specific waiver criteria showing need for nursing-facility-level supports that can safely be provided at home. The current authoritative policy is effective **Sept. 1, 2025**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## General CAP/DA eligibility criteria

A beneficiary must:

- **Be enrolled in NC Medicaid.** The policy says an eligible beneficiary must be enrolled in the NC Medicaid program, and some eligibility categories may still restrict access to this service. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)
- **Meet the general coverage standards in Section 3.1**, meaning the service must be:
  - individualized, specific, and consistent with the person’s symptoms or confirmed diagnosis;
  - safely furnished, with no equally effective and more conservative or less costly statewide alternative; and
  - not primarily for the convenience of the beneficiary, caregiver, or provider. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## CAP/DA-specific criteria

For CAP/DA waiver services, Medicaid covers the service when the beneficiary:

- **Meets the required HCBS nursing facility level of care**, as determined by NC Medicaid, and needs long-term-care support at a level typically provided in an institution, directly related to a documented medical diagnosis and functional care need;
- **Has a CAP assessment showing a reasonable indication of need** for at least one CAP/DA HCBS service within 30 calendar days of evaluation;
- **Has a signed service plan** authorizing one or more waiver services on a monthly basis to prevent institutionalization through coordinated case management;
- **Or has a signed short-term service plan** authorizing equipment, modification, or technology needed to return to or remain in the primary private residence and prevent institutional placement, with installation completed within three calendar months of service-plan approval;
- **Can maintain health, safety, and well-being** in the primary private residence or another approved location with formal and informal supports;
- **Has paid care needs that can be maintained within the average institutional cost** of the CAP/DA waiver;
- **Has a primary physician** who can link and coordinate medical services; and
- **Has an emergency backup and disaster plan** identifying who will provide care if key staff or the primary caregiver cannot. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## When CAP/DA is not covered

CAP/DA is not covered if the beneficiary does not meet the eligibility requirements in Section 2.0 or the coverage criteria in Section 3.0. The policy also lists specific noncoverage situations, including where required eligibility indicators or service request forms are missing, required assessments are incomplete or denied, the home environment is unsafe, or the beneficiary/responsible party refuses to sign or cooperate with the plan of care in a way that puts health or safety at risk. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

If you want, I can also turn this into a **short intake checklist** for screening CAP/DA eligibility.

Under **NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 10A**, the treatment plan (plan of care) for outpatient therapy services must include all of the following:

- the **start and end date** of the therapy treatment plan, with a duration of **no more than six months**;
- the **discipline-specific treatment diagnosis** and any related medical diagnoses;
- the beneficiary’s **rehabilitative or habilitative potential**;
- **defined goals** for each therapeutic discipline that are **specific, measurable, and reasonably expected to be achieved** within the plan period;
- the **skilled interventions, methodology, procedures, and specific programs** to be used;
- the **frequency of services**;
- the **length of each treatment visit in minutes**; and
- the **name, credentials, and signature** of the professional completing the treatment plan, **dated on or before the start date** of the plan. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) (effective Feb. 1, 2025), § 3.2.1.6.

Related documentation rule: the provider’s records must include **a copy of the treatment plan** as part of the service documentation. [Clinical Coverage Policy 10A](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/10a-outpatient-specialized-therapies/download?attachment) (effective Feb. 1, 2025), § 7.2.

I checked amendments for Policy 10A, and the current authoritative document is the Feb. 1, 2025 version cited above.

Yes. Under the current **CAP/DA policy, Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2**, a beneficiary **can transfer**, and the transfer must be coordinated so there are **no gaps in services**. The governing rule is **§ 3.3, “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries,”** in the current CAP/DA policy effective **Sept. 1, 2025**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Governing rule: transfers are allowed and must be coordinated

Policy **3K-2 § 3.3** says the **CME must coordinate the transfer to prevent gaps in service provisions**. Before the transfer, the following must be completed:

- determine the anticipated start date of service;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange a home visit within **5 business days** after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, so the receiving entity can assess the home environment and identify health and welfare concerns; and
- coordinate services so they **start on the first date of the transfer**.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

For a **CAP/DA beneficiary transferring to a different county**, § 3.3 adds that:

- the transferring CME and receiving CME coordinate the transfer on the agreed transfer date, or within **less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
- both agencies discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record is transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving CME arranges the home visit within **5 business days** of the move; and
- services must begin on the **first date of transfer** into the receiving county.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Supporting context: beneficiary choice and provider changes

Although § 3.3 is the transfer rule, **§ 5.4.2** supports that CAP/DA service planning must protect the beneficiary’s **right to choose among providers**, documented by a signed **Freedom of Choice** form. That section also requires the service plan to list the **authorized provider name, amount, frequency, and duration** of each service. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

That means transfers are not just administratively possible; the service plan and case-management process must reflect provider choice and continuity of authorized services.

## Provider-side rules relevant to transfers

If a new provider is taking over services, **§ 6.5** requires CAP/DA providers to:

- be registered in the **CAP Business System (e-CAP)**;
- **accept, decline, or acknowledge service authorizations within 3 business days** of submission in e-CAP;
- complete required CAP/DA training;
- attest to required safety and background-check policies.  
  [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

These requirements matter in practice because the receiving provider must be positioned to accept and implement the transferred authorization without a service interruption.

## Bottom line

A CAP/DA beneficiary **may transfer between service providers or between counties**, but the transfer is governed by **3K-2 § 3.3**, which requires coordinated transition planning, record transfer in e-CAP, health-and-safety planning, a prompt home visit after the move, and service start on the first transfer date so there is **no gap in services**. Beneficiary **freedom of choice of provider** in **§ 5.4.2** supports that rule. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

