The federal authority for NC’s CAP/DA waiver is **section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**, as implemented in **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. Under that authority, a state’s waiver request must show CMS that the waiver will provide home- and community-based services to people who would otherwise require institutional level of care, and it must include the required assurances and supporting documentation. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441); [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Federal rule

**42 CFR 441.300** states that **section 1915(c) of the Act permits states to offer, under a waiver of statutory requirements, home and community-based services an individual needs to avoid institutionalization**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

More specifically, **42 CFR 441.301** says a waiver request must include:

- the **assurances required by 42 CFR 441.302** and the **supporting documentation required by 42 CFR 441.303**;
- any requested waivers of certain Medicaid state-plan requirements, when applicable;
- a statement about whether the state will refuse HCBS when the cost would exceed institutional care;
- confirmation that services will be provided under a **written person-centered service plan** approved by the Medicaid agency;
- that services will be provided only to beneficiaries who are not institutional inpatients and who, **without the waiver services, would require the Medicaid-covered level of care in a hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID, as applicable**;
- descriptions of the responsible planners, target groups, and each waiver service as a separately defined service. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## What the state must demonstrate in its waiver application

Under **42 CFR 441.302** and **42 CFR 441.303**, the state must demonstrate enough to support CMS approval of the waiver. From the retrieved text, that includes at least the following:

### 1. Required assurances
The state must give CMS satisfactory assurances regarding matters including:

- **health and welfare protections** for beneficiaries;
- **provider standards** and compliance with state licensure/certification rules;
- **financial accountability** for waiver funds;
- services provided in **home and community-based settings**;
- an **incident management system** for critical incidents. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

### 2. Evaluation and level-of-care process
The state must document its plan for:

- **evaluation and reevaluation** of beneficiaries;
- who performs those evaluations and how;
- the forms used;
- maintaining written documentation; and
- regular reevaluations of need. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

And for diverted individuals, the state must use evaluation and screening procedures that ensure waiver services are **limited to people who otherwise would receive the applicable institutional level of care**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

### 3. Choice of HCBS versus institutional care
The state must describe how it will:

- inform eligible beneficiaries of the **feasible alternatives** under the waiver; and
- allow them to choose either **institutional services or home- and community-based services**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

### 4. Cost neutrality / expenditure limit
The state must provide an explanation, with supporting documentation satisfactory to CMS, showing **how it estimated average per-capita expenditures**, and the waiver must satisfy the cost-neutrality rule:

- the **annual average per-capita cost of waiver plus other Medicaid services for waiver participants must not exceed the estimated annual average per-capita cost of services those same individuals would receive absent the waiver**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

### 5. Number of participants
The state must indicate the **number of unduplicated beneficiaries** it intends to serve each year. That number operates as a limit unless the state later seeks and receives approval for a higher number through a waiver amendment. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## NC implementation context for CAP/DA

NC’s current CAP/DA clinical policy is the **current authoritative 3K-2 policy document**; I checked amendments for it and the tool returned no amendments, with the document itself as authoritative. The policy reflects the federal structure by requiring, for example, Medicaid eligibility and waiver-specific participation criteria. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

One retrieved NC section especially aligns with the federal waiver standard: **CAP/DA coverage requires that the beneficiary meet the required HCBS nursing facility level of care**, as shown in the search results for section 3.2.1. I did not retrieve the full text of that section, so I would not go beyond that wording. The NC policy also includes sections on person-centered service planning and mandated waiver assurances, but the retrieved text for section 7.15.2 itself was blank in the corpus, so I could not verify its detailed contents from the full text. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Note on public notice

Because you asked about the **waiver application**, one related federal rule is **42 CFR 431.408**, which requires a state public notice and comment process before submitting certain demonstration applications to CMS. That section was included in the routing frame for this question. But based on the text I retrieved for your specific question about CAP/DA waiver authority and application content, the more directly governing federal authority on what must be demonstrated in a **1915(c) HCBS waiver request** is **42 CFR 441.300, 441.301, 441.302, and 441.303**. [42 CFR Part 431](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-431); [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## Bottom line

For NC’s CAP/DA waiver, the federal statutory authority is **section 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**. In its waiver application, the state must demonstrate to CMS, through required assurances and supporting documentation, that:

- beneficiaries would otherwise need institutional level of care;
- services will be delivered under a person-centered service plan;
- health and welfare safeguards and provider standards are in place;
- beneficiaries are informed of alternatives and can choose HCBS or institutional care;
- the waiver is **cost-neutral** under the federal expenditure test; and
- the state has identified the number of people it intends to serve. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

