NC Medicaid does **not** cover ambulance services when the beneficiary or the service fails the policy’s basic coverage rules, and it also excludes several specific ambulance-related charges and situations.

## Not covered under the general rule
Ambulance services are not covered if:

- the beneficiary does **not** meet Medicaid eligibility requirements;
- the beneficiary does **not** meet the ambulance coverage criteria;
- the service **duplicates** another provider’s service; or
- the service is **experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial**.  
  [Ambulance Services, § 4.1](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment)

## Specific ambulance services and charges that are not covered
NC Medicaid specifically says the following are **not covered**:

- an ambulance is called and **no treatment is needed**;
- the ambulance responds to a **false alarm**;
- the beneficiary **refuses all medical services**;
- ambulance transport is for a **medical service that is not Medicaid-covered**;
- **commercial airline tickets**;
- **airstrip fees**;
- **taxes** charged separately;
- separate additional charges for **nursing personnel** who are employees of a facility or ambulance service;
- **waiting fees**;
- separate charges for **oxygen or other items/supplies**, because those are included in the base rate; and
- services other than those listed as covered in the policy.  
  [Ambulance Services, § 4.2.1.4](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment)

## Important limitation on destination
Even when ambulance transport is otherwise covered, NC Medicaid does **not** reimburse loaded mileage to a facility that is **not the nearest appropriate facility**. The patient’s or physician’s preference does not control that determination.  
[Ambulance Services, § 4.2.1.1](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment)

## NEMT manual distinction
The NEMT manual also distinguishes between:

- **medically necessary ambulance transportation**, which is not arranged by the local agency and is billed by the ambulance provider; and
- rare situations where an ambulance is the only available transport but is **not medically necessary**. In those cases, the local agency may arrange the transport, but the rate **must not include Basic Life Support (BLS) or Advanced Life Support (ALS) services**.  
  [MA-3550 NON-EMERGENCY MEDICAL TRANSPORTATION (NEMT), I. Ambulance Transportation that is not arranged by the local agency](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/MA-3550-.pdf); [MA-3550 NON-EMERGENCY MEDICAL TRANSPORTATION (NEMT), J. Ambulance Transportation arranged by the local agency](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/MA-3550-.pdf)

## Current-source note
The clinical policy I cited is current as retrieved: [Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment) (effective 12/15/2023).  
For the NEMT manual, amendment history shows change notices including [CHANGE NOTICE FOR MANUAL NO. 04-22, Non-Emergency Medical Transportation (NEMT)](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/cn-04-22.pdf). The corpus also shows MA-3550 as revised 03/16/2026.

