Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet both **initial/ongoing certification standards** in Clinical Coverage Policy 12B and the **recertification submission requirements** reflected in the DMA-3157 checklist. A provider can be **decertified** for a broad set of compliance failures, including failure to meet staffing, training, supervision, documentation, billing, confidentiality, legal, and certification standards. The policy I cite below appears current based on the amendment records returned for these documents.  
Citations: [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment); [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf)

## Certification requirements for HIV case management providers

### 1. Provider-level certification requirements
NC Medicaid policy 12B § 6.2.1 requires the provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers** meeting the qualifications in policy § 6.1.1, § 6.1.2, and § 6.1.3;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors** meeting § 6.1.4 and § 6.1.5;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV case management provider;
- meet applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation laws**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV case management provider;
- demonstrate compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- demonstrate compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary records and inspect agency operations and financial records**;
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed changes such as ownership, name, address, phone number, or dissolution **within 30 calendar days of the proposed change and no later than five business days after the actual change**; and
- **within one year of Medicaid enrollment**, achieve national accreditation from an approved accrediting body: ACHC, CARF, CHAP, COA, PHAB, The Joint Commission, or URAC.  
Citation: Policy 12B § 6.2.1 in [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (effective 2020-01-15; no amendment returned).

### 2. Administrative and operational requirements tied to certification
The policy also includes administrative requirements relevant to certification, including that the agency must:

- have a **physical business site** at the time of application, verified by site visit, and the site **cannot be in a private residence or vehicle**;
- submit an **organizational chart**;
- disclose persons with **5% or more ownership**;
- obtain and maintain access to required **web-based applications**;
- submit a **business plan** with development costs, projected revenue/expenses, prior actual revenue/expenses, management information, and existing financial resources;
- have the **computer capability** to maintain electronic records, meet HIPAA/security standards, perform data analysis, comply with electronic billing and EFT requirements, and communicate monthly with CCNC Network or the primary care provider as required by policy;
- complete a **precertification onsite visit**;
- meet all applicable **state and federal licensure and certification requirements**; and
- maintain written policies on confidentiality, grievances, rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflicts, electronic records, medical records, freedom of choice, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation.  
Citation: administrative requirements section in [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment) (same current document).

### 3. Recertification checklist items
The DMA-3157 recertification checklist shows NC Medicaid expects recertification submissions to include, at minimum:

- application for **one physical location**;
- title page and **signed Recertification Application Checklist**;
- if mailed, a **bound submission**; if electronic, the application and related documents scanned as **one file**;
- complete agency demographics and contact information;
- lists of all **case managers** with employment dates;
- lists of all **case management supervisors** with employment dates;
- all **counties** where HIV case management services are provided;
- all **services** provided by the agency;
- **operating hours** and the **emergency after-hours plan**;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- the frequency of the **satisfaction survey** and how results are used; and
- the numbers of **Medicaid and Ryan White clients**.  
Citation: [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (current per amendment lookup).

## When a provider can be decertified

Policy 12B § 6.2.5 says an HIV case management provider may be decertified for any of the following:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** within **30 calendar days** to resolve unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet the qualifications in **§ 6.1**;
- failure of staff to complete mandatory **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units** under § 6.1;
- failure to provide case management staff with adequate **supervision** to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within the required **time frame** set by policy, NC Medicaid, or its designated contractor;
- failure to maintain documentation under **§ 7.5** sufficient to support billing;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its contractor of changes in agency name, director, ownership, mailing address, or telephone number, where that prevents contact;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; and
- failure to achieve or maintain the **certification requirements in § 6.2**.  
Citation: Policy 12B § 6.2.5 in [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment).

## Documentation-related risks that can lead to decertification
Because § 6.2.5 expressly ties decertification to failures in required documentation, two other sections matter:

- **§ 7.5** requires records supporting intake, assessments, care plans, progress notes, physician/practitioner orders, time and units, contacts, consent forms, and achievement of care-plan goals.
- **§ 7.6** imposes deadlines, including initial assessment within **five business days** of referral, care plan within **five business days** after assessment/reassessment, progress notes within **24 hours** of contact, periodic contact and care-plan review requirements, annual satisfaction survey, and advance written notice before termination or change in services.  
Citations: Policy 12B § 7.5 and § 7.6 in [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment).

## Bottom line
To stay certified, an HIV case management provider must maintain qualified staff and supervision, enroll each site, meet legal and Medicaid participation requirements, maintain accreditation, cooperate with oversight, keep timely and sufficient records, and comply with quality-improvement and notice obligations. A provider can be decertified if it falls out of compliance with those requirements or engages in fraud, falsification, confidentiality violations, staffing/training failures, or documentation and billing deficiencies.

